  "title": "Bromine and water convert propene to 1-bromopropan-2-ol, water opening the bromonium at the more substituted carbon and bromide taking the proton off",
  "expect": {
    "kind": "good",
    "note": "The same bromonium as good-bromine-addition-to-cis-2-butene-via-a-bromonium-ion, opened by a different nucleophile, which is why the halohydrin is anti and Markovnikov at once: anti because the ring is opened from the back, Markovnikov because the more substituted carbon carries more of the positive charge in the unsymmetrical bridge and so is the one attacked. The regiochemistry is a reactivity claim and no check here endorses it. Bromide, not a second water, takes the proton in step 3, so the byproduct is HBr and the multiset never contains a species that was not drawn."
